    "evidence_text": "Haematopoietic stem cells do not asymmetrically segregate chromosomes or retain BrdU\n\nHere we tested these hypotheses in haematopoietic stem cells (HSCs), which can be highly purified using well characterized markers.\n\nDivision of individual HSCs in culture revealed no asymmetric segregation of the label.\n\nHematopoietic stem cell: self-renewal versus differentiation.\n\nWithin the system, only HSCs possess the ability of both multipotency and self-renewal.\n\nMultipotency is the ability to differentiate into all functional blood cells.\n\nSelf-renewal is the ability to give rise to HSC itself without differentiation.\n\nThus, understanding the mechanisms of self-renewal and differentiation of HSC has been a central issue.\n\nCombined Single-Cell Functional and Gene Expression Analysis Resolves Heterogeneity within Stem Cell Populations\n\nFinally, we demonstrated the broader applicability of this approach for linking key molecules with defined cellular functions in another stem cell system.\n\nAsymmetric Cell Divisions Sustain Long-Term  Hematopoiesis from Single-sorted Human Fetal Liver Cells \n\nSingle cells in slow growing colonies were recloned upon reaching a 100\u2013200 cell stage.\n\nStrikingly, the number of cells in subclones varied widely again.\n\nIn vivo proliferation and cell cycle kinetics of long-term self-renewing hematopoietic stem cells.\n\nA rare set of hematopoietic stem cells (HSC) must undergo a massive expansion to produce mature blood cells.\n\nThe phenotypic isolation of HSC from mice offers the opportunity to determine directly their proliferation kinetics.\n\nBy 6 months, 99% of LT-HSC had incorporated BrdUrd.\n\nWe calculated that approximately 8% of LT-HSC asynchronously entered the cell cycle per day.\n\nNested reverse transcription-PCR analysis revealed cyclin D2 expression in a high proportion of LT-HSC.\n\nAngiopoietin-like proteins stimulate ex vivo expansion of hematopoietic stem cells\n\nPurification and characterization of mouse hematopoietic stem cells.\nMouse bone marrow hematopoietic stem cells were isolated with the use of a variety of phenotypic markers.\nThese cells can proliferate and differentiate with approximately unit efficiency into myelomonocytic cells, B cells, or T cells.\nThirty of these cells are sufficient to save 50 percent of lethally irradiated mice, and to reconstitute all blood cell types in the survivors.\n\nVery small embryonic-like stem cells with maximum regenerative potential get discarded during cord blood banking and bone marrow processing for autologous stem cell therapy.\n\nHSCs or MSCs, which follow differentiation trajectories depending on the somatic niche.\n\nHES-1 preserves purified hematopoietic stem cells ex vivo and accumulates side population cells in vivo.\n\nNodal/Activin signaling predicts human pluripotent stem cell lines prone to differentiate toward the hematopoietic lineage.\n\nCorrelation studies revealed that the CFU potential robustly correlates with hemogenic progenitors and primitive but not mature blood cells.\n\nMicroarray studies showed distinct gene expression profile between hPSCs with good versus poor hematopoietic potential.",
